WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
(jazzy music)

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:03.416
- [Narrator] Welcome to Norway.

00:00:04.409 --> 00:00:06.963
Norway, the western part of Scandinavia,

00:00:06.963 --> 00:00:09.208
It was established in the year 872.

00:00:09.208 --> 00:00:12.180
The beautiful country is home to vikings,

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.549
an influential black metal scene,

00:00:14.549 --> 00:00:18.716
a large nationalized oil
company and these arctic foxes.

00:00:20.226 --> 00:00:21.515
So cute.

00:00:21.515 --> 00:00:23.686
So while Norway has a
long and storied history,

00:00:23.686 --> 00:00:25.729
let's focus on 2009.

00:00:25.729 --> 00:00:28.690
No cultural event would
compare to the gargantuan

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:32.773
collossal, mammoth, very
large in size phenomenon

00:00:34.449 --> 00:00:37.444
of this train traveling
from Oslo to Bergen,

00:00:37.444 --> 00:00:40.361
televised for seven hours straight.

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:43.363
This is slow TV.

00:00:44.336 --> 00:00:45.790
(upbeat music)

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:47.369
Slow TV is...

00:00:47.369 --> 00:00:49.369
- [Thomas] Slow TV is...

00:00:50.932 --> 00:00:52.682
A way of telling stories

00:00:52.682 --> 00:00:54.066
- [Narrator] that is...

00:00:54.066 --> 00:00:57.482
- [Thomas] told in full length,
along an unbroken timeline.

00:00:57.482 --> 00:00:58.315
- [Narrator] This guy works at

00:00:58.315 --> 00:01:00.173
the Norwegian Broadcasting Company.

00:01:00.173 --> 00:01:01.433
My name is Thomas Hellum.

00:01:01.433 --> 00:01:03.685
I'm working as a sort of project manager,

00:01:03.685 --> 00:01:06.599
currently working a lot with real slow,

00:01:06.599 --> 00:01:09.235
slow, slow (slurred to
slow motion), slow TV.

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:10.068
- [Narrator] He helped create a type

00:01:10.068 --> 00:01:11.940
of television series that shows...

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.355
- [Thomas] a subject
that is somehow rooted

00:01:14.355 --> 00:01:18.442
in Norwegian culture or that
many people can relate to.

00:01:18.442 --> 00:01:20.675
- [Narrator] So some
examples are marathon long

00:01:20.675 --> 00:01:21.737
episodes of people...

00:01:21.737 --> 00:01:23.990
- [Thomas] Salmon fishing, 18 hours.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:26.497
It took three hours before
we got the first fish.

00:01:26.497 --> 00:01:29.795
- [Narrator] 60 hours of a
choir performing 899 hymns

00:01:29.795 --> 00:01:31.002
from the Book of Norway.

00:01:31.002 --> 00:01:32.674
- [Thomas] We had the
national firewood night,

00:01:32.674 --> 00:01:35.292
with a fireplace burning for eight hours.

00:01:35.292 --> 00:01:36.511
- [Narrator] You get the idea,

00:01:36.511 --> 00:01:38.926
but how did Thomas come upon the idea.

00:01:38.926 --> 00:01:41.469
- [Thomas] Uh, this was
actually done by accident.

00:01:41.469 --> 00:01:44.219
(horn crescendo)

00:01:45.474 --> 00:01:47.390
- [Narrator] Well so in
2009, Thomas is at lunch

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:49.773
with some colleagues,
spitballing some ideas

00:01:49.773 --> 00:01:51.267
and had discussions over...

00:01:51.267 --> 00:01:54.193
- [Thomas] Topics that take
long time, and other stories

00:01:54.193 --> 00:01:57.235
that you could sort of
tell as they evolves.

00:01:57.235 --> 00:01:58.524
- [Narrator] After a lot of talk,

00:01:58.524 --> 00:02:00.652
- [Thomas] One of us just
said that why don't we film

00:02:00.652 --> 00:02:03.578
the whole train journey
from western part of Norway

00:02:03.578 --> 00:02:04.773
to eastern part of Norway.

00:02:04.773 --> 00:02:05.749
It takes seven hours,

00:02:05.749 --> 00:02:09.127
and why don't we just film the whole thing

00:02:09.127 --> 00:02:10.544
and put it up on TV.

00:02:10.544 --> 00:02:12.164
- [Narrator] They went to their bosses.

00:02:12.164 --> 00:02:14.208
- [Thomas] First they laughed
and didn't quite understand

00:02:14.208 --> 00:02:15.708
what we suggested.

00:02:16.959 --> 00:02:19.379
But after have laughed a little bit,

00:02:19.379 --> 00:02:22.467
- [Narrator] They realized that
maybe this was a good idea.

00:02:22.467 --> 00:02:25.057
So they got to work and planned it out.

00:02:25.057 --> 00:02:28.435
And on November 27th, 2009, the experiment

00:02:28.435 --> 00:02:30.525
was released on air.

00:02:30.525 --> 00:02:31.895
To Thomas' surprise,

00:02:31.895 --> 00:02:34.228
- [Thomas] 1.6 million Norwegian watched

00:02:34.228 --> 00:02:35.520
part of that program.

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:37.110
- [Narrator] So much people.

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:40.071
- [Thomas] And after that, we
thought, hey, this is a way

00:02:40.071 --> 00:02:42.323
we can tell other stories, as well.

00:02:42.323 --> 00:02:43.449
- [Narrator] The next production was going

00:02:43.449 --> 00:02:44.738
to be even bigger.

00:02:44.738 --> 00:02:46.747
A five and a half day boat trip going

00:02:46.747 --> 00:02:50.206
along the Norwegian coast,
televised in real time.

00:02:50.206 --> 00:02:53.237
- [Thomas] And we transmitted
the world's longest

00:02:53.237 --> 00:02:56.905
TV documentary unbroken,
no news, no commercials,

00:02:56.905 --> 00:03:00.795
no nothing, except this journey
for five and a half days.

00:03:00.795 --> 00:03:04.719
And 3.2 million Norwegian
follow that journey.

00:03:04.719 --> 00:03:05.845
- [Narrator] More than
half of the population

00:03:05.845 --> 00:03:08.388
of Norway tuned in at some
point in the broadcast.

00:03:08.388 --> 00:03:11.302
But why? What made this show so popular?

00:03:11.302 --> 00:03:13.067
- [Thomas] Part of it,
of course, it's that

00:03:13.067 --> 00:03:15.690
it was something completely different.

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:19.940
The TV was no longer a TV
screen, but sort of a window

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:23.307
as they were actually doing the journey,

00:03:23.307 --> 00:03:26.406
as they were on that ship or on that train.

00:03:26.406 --> 00:03:27.904
- [Narrator] After eight
years, Thomas and crew

00:03:27.904 --> 00:03:29.773
are still pushing the envelope.

00:03:29.773 --> 00:03:33.036
- [Thomas] If somebody had
told me that eight years ago

00:03:33.036 --> 00:03:36.240
that hey, you're going to
make a 12 hour TV program,

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:39.823
I would've said they
were completely crazy.

